From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Notify observers of the keys an array controller selection affects

-[NSArrayController setSelectionIndexes:] changes the selection without
a word to key-value observers. Automatic KVO fires for selectionIndexes
itself, since it is the setter, but nothing is declared to depend on it,
so selection, selectedObjects, canRemove and canSelectNext/Previous stay
silent. Every binding that follows the selection freezes at its initial
state: a Remove button bound to canRemove stays grey forever, and a
control bound through selection.<key> keeps the first row's value.

-selection also answered with the raw content array, so selection.<key>
collected over every row and could not be observed at all, since
observing a key on an array raises.

Declare the dependent keys with +keyPathsForValuesAffecting..., and
answer -selection with the selected object when exactly one row is
selected and nil otherwise. Full Cocoa fidelity wants a multi-selection
proxy answering NSMultipleValuesMarker; that is noted in the code for
the day a binding needs it.

A reproduction program accompanies this patch; it registers observers
for the seven selection-dependent key paths, changes the selection and
reports which of them heard about it - exit 0 with the change, 1
without.
---
diff --git a/Source/NSArrayController.m b/Source/NSArrayController.m
index 471dee4..fbb8ad9 100644
--- a/Source/NSArrayController.m
+++ b/Source/NSArrayController.m
@@ -397,6 +397,64 @@
   return [[self arrangedObjects] objectsAtIndexes: _selection_indexes];
 }
 
+/* Everything a selection-dependent binding observes is derived from
+ * selectionIndexes (and, for the derived values below, the content),
+ * and setSelectionIndexes: already emits automatic KVO for its own
+ * key: declaring the dependencies makes selection, selectedObjects,
+ * canRemove and canSelectNext/Previous notify too, which is what keeps
+ * an enabled binding on canRemove or a value binding through
+ * selection.<key> alive when the user clicks another row. */
++ (NSSet*) keyPathsForValuesAffectingSelection
+{
+  return [NSSet setWithObjects: @"selectionIndexes", @"content", nil];
+}
+
++ (NSSet*) keyPathsForValuesAffectingSelectedObjects
+{
+  return [NSSet setWithObjects: @"selectionIndexes", @"content", nil];
+}
+
++ (NSSet*) keyPathsForValuesAffectingSelectionIndex
+{
+  return [NSSet setWithObject: @"selectionIndexes"];
+}
+
++ (NSSet*) keyPathsForValuesAffectingCanRemove
+{
+  return [NSSet setWithObject: @"selectionIndexes"];
+}
+
++ (NSSet*) keyPathsForValuesAffectingCanSelectNext
+{
+  return [NSSet setWithObjects: @"selectionIndexes", @"content", nil];
+}
+
++ (NSSet*) keyPathsForValuesAffectingCanSelectPrevious
+{
+  return [NSSet setWithObjects: @"selectionIndexes", @"content", nil];
+}
+
+/* The object selection.<key> paths read and write through.  The
+ * inherited implementation answers with the content ARRAY, so
+ * selection.name collected over every row (and observing it raised,
+ * because a key cannot be observed on an array).  One selected object
+ * is the overwhelmingly common binding case and is returned as
+ * itself; no selection, and - for now - multiple selection, answer
+ * nil, which key-value coding and the binding placeholders both
+ * handle.  (Full Cocoa fidelity wants a proxy that answers
+ * NSMultipleValuesMarker per key for multiple selection; that is left
+ * for the day a binding here needs it.) */
+- (id) selection
+{
+  NSArray *selected = [self selectedObjects];
+
+  if ([selected count] == 1)
+    {
+      return [selected objectAtIndex: 0];
+    }
+  return nil;
+}
+
 - (NSUInteger) selectionIndex
 {
   return [_selection_indexes firstIndex];
